1
00:00:02,282 --> 00:00:05,617
Let's write this new script. I'll name it

2
00:00:06,712 --> 00:00:09,096
fetchWebContent.ts.

3
00:00:10,680 --> 00:00:11,325
That's my file name.

4
00:00:12,407 --> 00:00:14,327
And in there we got a couple of steps. We

5
00:00:14,327 --> 00:00:16,280
want to fetch user input. We learned how

6
00:00:16,345 --> 00:00:18,847
that works. We want to write to a file. We

7
00:00:18,847 --> 00:00:19,702
learned how that works.

8
00:00:21,220 --> 00:00:23,336
But in between, I also want to send a web

9
00:00:23,336 --> 00:00:24,883
request, a network request.

10
00:00:25,723 --> 00:00:27,885
So first of all, let's create that buffer

11
00:00:28,062 --> 00:00:30,014
for collecting the user input, and that's a

12
00:00:30,078 --> 00:00:31,966
new uint8 array.

13
00:00:33,850 --> 00:00:34,560
And thereafter,

14
00:00:35,254 --> 00:00:39,257
we can call deno.stdin.read and pass our

15
00:00:39,289 --> 00:00:40,112
buffer to read.

16
00:00:42,544 --> 00:00:44,460
And I'm going to close the deno.input.ts

17
00:00:44,460 --> 00:00:47,166
file to help my IDE understand that this

18
00:00:47,697 --> 00:00:49,823
buffer is not redeclared but in a brand new

19
00:00:49,871 --> 00:00:50,064
file.

20
00:00:50,982 --> 00:00:52,639
Now I also wanna give this a length.

21
00:00:53,216 --> 00:00:53,745
Of again

22
00:00:54,659 --> 00:00:56,455
1024 bytes.

23
00:00:57,385 --> 00:00:59,791
And again, we get back a promise here, so,

24
00:01:00,143 --> 00:01:02,789
so I'll await that promise. Thereafter, we

25
00:01:02,789 --> 00:01:05,002
know that our buffer is populated with the

26
00:01:05,002 --> 00:01:07,135
user input. So then we can create our

27
00:01:07,328 --> 00:01:08,274
decoder again,

28
00:01:09,733 --> 00:01:11,481
our new TextDecoder,

29
00:01:12,299 --> 00:01:15,201
to then get the URL which the user should

30
00:01:15,266 --> 00:01:15,650
enter here

31
00:01:16,949 --> 00:01:19,611
by calling decoder.decode buffer.

32
00:01:20,413 --> 00:01:23,219
Now to help the user understand what he or

33
00:01:23,219 --> 00:01:25,064
she should enter, I'm also going to

34
00:01:25,064 --> 00:01:26,092
console.log,

35
00:01:27,312 --> 00:01:29,591
please enter a URL here,

36
00:01:30,457 --> 00:01:32,849
to make it clear that we, well, want a URL

37
00:01:32,994 --> 00:01:33,459
as input.

38
00:01:34,133 --> 00:01:36,107
And then here we got the URL, hopefully.

39
00:01:36,846 --> 00:01:39,574
Now we wanna send a network request to that

40
00:01:39,574 --> 00:01:39,944
URL.

41
00:01:40,698 --> 00:01:42,801
So for that, we can use the Fetch API.

42
00:01:43,346 --> 00:01:45,690
As I mentioned, this is available in Deno.

43
00:01:46,235 --> 00:01:48,338
It has this browser compatibility.

44
00:01:49,542 --> 00:01:53,249
So Fetch indeed wants a URL which can be a

45
00:01:53,249 --> 00:01:55,958
string as a first parameter. So I can just

46
00:01:55,958 --> 00:01:57,465
pass my URL to it.

47
00:01:58,684 --> 00:02:00,495
And we could configure this request in

48
00:02:00,592 --> 00:02:03,381
greater detail with a second options object

49
00:02:03,814 --> 00:02:05,818
where we could change the request method,

50
00:02:06,139 --> 00:02:07,886
but the default will be a GET request and

51
00:02:07,918 --> 00:02:10,067
I'm just fine with that. So I'll send a GET

52
00:02:10,067 --> 00:02:10,580
request here

53
00:02:11,349 --> 00:02:14,235
and fetch also returns a promise where we

54
00:02:14,299 --> 00:02:16,367
get back our response object.

55
00:02:17,682 --> 00:02:20,552
So here we can store the response in a

56
00:02:20,552 --> 00:02:23,581
constant named response, awaiting fetch.

57
00:02:24,174 --> 00:02:26,081
This will send the request and give us the

58
00:02:26,081 --> 00:02:26,738
response.

59
00:02:27,699 --> 00:02:30,119
Now the response is an object that holds a

60
00:02:30,183 --> 00:02:33,371
bunch of data, and amongst that data we'll

61
00:02:33,371 --> 00:02:36,303
have the, the response body, so the core

62
00:02:36,432 --> 00:02:38,803
data that was enclosed in the response.

63
00:02:39,636 --> 00:02:42,360
To get that, we need to call response dot,

64
00:02:43,001 --> 00:02:43,514
and then here

65
00:02:44,523 --> 00:02:45,661
we can call text.

66
00:02:46,206 --> 00:02:48,176
This is a method which will parse the

67
00:02:48,208 --> 00:02:51,589
response body as text. So this again gives

68
00:02:51,589 --> 00:02:54,121
us a promise which then ultimately returns a

69
00:02:54,121 --> 00:02:54,698
string though.

70
00:02:55,419 --> 00:02:58,448
Now if you plan on entering a URL that

71
00:02:58,448 --> 00:03:01,220
fetches, let's say, JSON data, you can call

72
00:03:01,429 --> 00:03:04,121
JSON instead to parse that JSON code

73
00:03:04,137 --> 00:03:04,601
correctly.

74
00:03:05,515 --> 00:03:07,534
Now of course we could also write our script

75
00:03:07,534 --> 00:03:08,752
in a more flexible way

76
00:03:09,425 --> 00:03:11,668
where we try to detect which kind of data we

77
00:03:11,668 --> 00:03:14,617
got back, but I will hardcode this to text

78
00:03:14,905 --> 00:03:17,133
so that I'm able to deal with text data.

79
00:03:18,575 --> 00:03:19,344
Now this also,

80
00:03:20,001 --> 00:03:22,004
as I mentioned, gives us a promise, so I

81
00:03:22,004 --> 00:03:23,942
will await it, and then I got my

82
00:03:25,144 --> 00:03:27,355
content here, or however you wanna name it.

83
00:03:28,028 --> 00:03:29,903
And that should now be saved to a file.

84
00:03:30,928 --> 00:03:33,733
So that's the last step. We can use

85
00:03:33,861 --> 00:03:35,751
deno.writeFile

86
00:03:36,761 --> 00:03:38,171
to write to a file,

87
00:03:38,908 --> 00:03:41,872
and as a first argument, enter the path to

88
00:03:41,872 --> 00:03:43,779
the file including the file name.

89
00:03:44,698 --> 00:03:45,716
And I will choose

90
00:03:47,122 --> 00:03:53,184
website-content.txt as a file name here, but

91
00:03:53,201 --> 00:03:54,283
the name is of course up to you.

92
00:03:55,541 --> 00:03:56,799
And the second argument should be the

93
00:03:56,799 --> 00:03:57,719
content, but

94
00:03:58,348 --> 00:04:00,719
the content as a Uint8Array.

95
00:04:01,572 --> 00:04:04,051
So we first of all need to encode this

96
00:04:04,051 --> 00:04:05,435
content to such an array,

97
00:04:06,079 --> 00:04:08,398
and for that I'll create my encoder by

98
00:04:08,398 --> 00:04:09,525
calling new TextEncoder.

99
00:04:10,880 --> 00:04:12,956
And then get my data by calling

100
00:04:13,134 --> 00:04:16,361
encoder.encode and passing in the content.

101
00:04:17,356 --> 00:04:18,915
And now it's that data that should be

102
00:04:19,043 --> 00:04:20,634
written to that file.

103
00:04:21,952 --> 00:04:23,978
And then we can again await this and

104
00:04:23,978 --> 00:04:26,019
thereafter console.log done.

105
00:04:28,120 --> 00:04:30,149
Theoretically, this should work. Now let's

106
00:04:30,149 --> 00:04:32,082
give it a try. Let's deno run

107
00:04:32,984 --> 00:04:36,931
the fetchWebContent.ts file.

108
00:04:37,832 --> 00:04:39,910
And if we run it like this, you will see

109
00:04:40,105 --> 00:04:41,128
that it wants a URL.

110
00:04:41,762 --> 00:04:43,645
So here indeed I am going to enter

111
00:04:43,645 --> 00:04:44,831
google.com,

112
00:04:45,480 --> 00:04:45,916
let's say.

113
00:04:47,534 --> 00:04:48,844
But then I get an error,

114
00:04:49,620 --> 00:04:51,836
and again I get a permission denied error.

115
00:04:52,532 --> 00:04:54,425
And it's also telling me which permission

116
00:04:54,489 --> 00:04:57,295
was denied. Network access was the problem.

117
00:04:57,537 --> 00:04:59,778
It also gives me the solution. We should add

118
00:04:59,891 --> 00:05:00,794
allow net.

119
00:05:02,205 --> 00:05:04,318
Remember, permissions are an important thing

120
00:05:04,495 --> 00:05:06,753
in Deno, and here we plan on working with

121
00:05:06,753 --> 00:05:06,947
the network.

122
00:05:08,012 --> 00:05:10,916
Hence, we need to add the allow net flag

123
00:05:10,916 --> 00:05:13,353
when we execute the script. We could also

124
00:05:13,482 --> 00:05:15,419
lock it down to specific domains if we

125
00:05:15,435 --> 00:05:15,903
wanted to.

126
00:05:16,820 --> 00:05:19,224
Side note, since we'll also write to a file

127
00:05:19,353 --> 00:05:22,629
thereafter, we should also add --allow-write

128
00:05:22,725 --> 00:05:23,290
as a flag.

129
00:05:24,660 --> 00:05:26,841
So here I'm going to deno run

130
00:05:28,796 --> 00:05:29,022
with

131
00:05:29,927 --> 00:05:32,706
--allow-write and --allow-net.

132
00:05:34,322 --> 00:05:36,886
And I will not restrict the network access

133
00:05:36,886 --> 00:05:39,323
because I typically don't know in advance

134
00:05:39,371 --> 00:05:41,407
which URL the user is going to enter.

135
00:05:42,352 --> 00:05:44,212
So now thereafter, I'll add the file name

136
00:05:44,340 --> 00:05:45,350
and execute this again.

137
00:05:46,584 --> 00:05:48,428
And now I'm trying to fetch data from

138
00:05:48,508 --> 00:05:49,101
google.com.

139
00:05:49,630 --> 00:05:52,563
I get done, I got my website content, and

140
00:05:52,595 --> 00:05:55,208
that looks quite good. We got some HTML code

141
00:05:55,320 --> 00:05:58,671
in there, and that very likely is the HTML

142
00:05:58,671 --> 00:05:58,959
code

143
00:05:59,488 --> 00:06:01,235
that is used for google.com.

144
00:06:02,133 --> 00:06:04,968
If we visit google.com and we view the page

145
00:06:05,080 --> 00:06:05,497
source,

146
00:06:06,218 --> 00:06:09,550
there we have way more than what was loaded.

147
00:06:10,063 --> 00:06:12,082
But chances are that a lot of this content

148
00:06:12,082 --> 00:06:15,158
is actually loaded only after the page was

149
00:06:15,174 --> 00:06:16,440
loaded for the first time,

150
00:06:17,097 --> 00:06:19,227
and/or that the content we're seeing here

151
00:06:19,820 --> 00:06:22,095
is some content optimized for automated

152
00:06:22,095 --> 00:06:25,043
scripts or crawlers. And in addition, if you

153
00:06:25,043 --> 00:06:27,575
scroll to the side, you see we actually did

154
00:06:27,607 --> 00:06:29,465
fetch quite a lot of content. So this

155
00:06:29,513 --> 00:06:32,713
definitely is the Google content. Of course,

156
00:06:32,778 --> 00:06:35,104
we could have also stored it in a file that

157
00:06:35,200 --> 00:06:37,930
ends with.html if we wanted to create an

158
00:06:38,027 --> 00:06:38,802
HTML file.

159
00:06:39,351 --> 00:06:41,599
If I now run this script again with this

160
00:06:41,599 --> 00:06:42,200
changed file

161
00:06:42,817 --> 00:06:43,580
extension here,

162
00:06:44,912 --> 00:06:45,285
and I now

163
00:06:45,821 --> 00:06:47,802
again fetch the content from google.com,

164
00:06:48,715 --> 00:06:51,424
you see I got this HTML file here. And if I

165
00:06:51,473 --> 00:06:53,415
reformat this with my IDE,

166
00:06:55,238 --> 00:06:57,436
this really looks like HTML code to me.

167
00:06:58,147 --> 00:07:00,490
So that is how we can fetch data from a

168
00:07:00,490 --> 00:07:03,487
website with Deno. And that's another nice

169
00:07:03,536 --> 00:07:06,506
practice of using the Deno core APIs and

170
00:07:06,602 --> 00:07:09,799
understanding how Deno works and what Deno

171
00:07:09,912 --> 00:07:10,589
is about.
